+# Proof obligations and scope
+
+| Formulation | Mechanism | Terminal obligation | Strength/status | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Quadratic action | Euler variation and positive Hessian | correct source sign, three configuration modes | component / candidate-complete | complete action and energy proof |
+| Spatial matrix | longitudinal/transverse projectors | tensor inverse and scalar contraction | component / candidate-complete | symbolic identity and direct derivation |
+| Local conservation law | sharp stress-energy flux bound | compact full-data domain of dependence | component / candidate-complete | quadratic-form Cauchy-Schwarz proof |
+| Retarded distribution | shell mass cancellation | full Green tensor and finite support | component / candidate-complete | radial Poisson inversion and independent tensor quadrature |
+| Zero-frequency limit | integrate both fronts and intervening term | recover static fundamental solution | component / candidate-complete | analytic time integral |
+| Nonlinear source and dynamical background | not supplied | recover source-dependent response under one local action | stronger / unexplored | explicit next target, not hidden inside the linear proof |
+
+The linear theorem has an acyclic proof graph: the supplied action gives the
+Euler equation, its spatial spectrum gives the retarded Fourier solution,
+radial inversion establishes its real tensor, and either energy or the explicit
+kernel proves propagation. Static inversion and retarded integration agree.
+The nonlinear physical reconstruction is a stronger distinct task, not a
+renamed lemma claimed to be almost proved.
